1
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:06,756
FDMA and TDMA are the two answers to the
oldest question in radio: how do many

2
00:00:06,756 --> 00:00:11,162
conversations share one band without
talking over each other?

3
00:00:11,512 --> 00:00:14,623
The first answer is frequency-division
multiple access.

4
00:00:14,743 --> 00:00:17,541
Slice the band into non-overlapping
channels, and give each call its own

5
00:00:17,541 --> 00:00:20,339
frequency for the whole duration.

6
00:00:20,459 --> 00:00:23,326
Call A on one channel, call B on the next,
small guard bands between them so

7
00:00:23,326 --> 00:00:26,194
neighbors don't interfere.

8
00:00:26,315 --> 00:00:30,599
Capacity scales the obvious way: more
conversations means more channels.

9
00:00:30,949 --> 00:00:34,426
Its virtue is simplicity — no timing to
coordinate, tolerant of distance and delay

10
00:00:34,426 --> 00:00:37,903
— which is why it carried early
land-mobile radio.

11
00:00:38,023 --> 00:00:41,715
Its cost is that a quiet talker still ties
up a whole frequency.

12
00:00:41,835 --> 00:00:47,060
So the industry narrowed the slices
instead: twenty-five kilohertz FM became

13
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:52,284
the twelve-and-a-half of P25 Phase 1, and
the six-and-a-quarter of NXDN.

14
00:00:52,634 --> 00:00:55,026
The second answer divides time instead.

15
00:00:55,146 --> 00:00:58,809
Time-division multiple access splits one
frequency into rapid, repeating timeslots,

16
00:00:58,809 --> 00:01:02,472
and calls take turns.

17
00:01:02,592 --> 00:01:07,689
Call one bursts in slot one, call two in
slot two, back to call one — many times

18
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:12,786
per second, fast enough that each
conversation sounds perfectly continuous.

19
00:01:12,905 --> 00:01:15,053
One channel now does the work of two.

20
00:01:15,403 --> 00:01:16,587
The price is discipline.

21
00:01:16,707 --> 00:01:20,151
Every radio must burst exactly inside its
slot, so bursts are padded with a little

22
00:01:20,151 --> 00:01:23,596
guard time, and receivers lock onto a
frame-synchronization pattern to learn

23
00:01:23,596 --> 00:01:27,040
where the slot boundaries fall.

24
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:31,548
In exchange, DMR and P25 Phase 2 double
the calls per frequency with two slots,

25
00:01:31,548 --> 00:01:35,937
and TETRA packs four calls onto one
carrier.

26
00:01:36,057 --> 00:01:38,582
A subscriber radio even saves battery by
switching its transmitter off between

27
00:01:38,582 --> 00:01:41,107
bursts.

28
00:01:41,458 --> 00:01:44,250
Real systems combine both: FDMA
channelizes the band, TDMA time-shares

29
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:47,042
each channel.

30
00:01:47,162 --> 00:01:51,568
And for trunking, the difference shows up
in the grant itself — on FDMA a grant

31
00:01:51,568 --> 00:01:55,974
names a frequency; on TDMA it must name a
frequency and a slot.

32
00:01:56,324 --> 00:01:58,762
For an SDR, the split is concrete.

33
00:01:58,882 --> 00:02:03,230
On FDMA, a granted channel is simply a
carrier: GopherTrunk down-converts that

34
00:02:03,230 --> 00:02:07,578
frequency from the wideband capture and
demodulates it.

35
00:02:07,698 --> 00:02:13,294
On TDMA, the same carrier holds two or
four unrelated calls, so GopherTrunk must

36
00:02:13,294 --> 00:02:18,890
also recover burst timing, sync to the
frame, and pull out just the granted slot

37
00:02:18,890 --> 00:02:24,486
— which is exactly what its DMR, P25 Phase
2, and TETRA decode paths do.

38
00:02:25,636 --> 00:02:30,911
So: FDMA gives each call its own
frequency; TDMA lets calls share one

39
00:02:30,911 --> 00:02:36,185
frequency in timeslots, doubling or
quadrupling capacity at the cost of tight

40
00:02:36,185 --> 00:02:41,460
timing — and on TDMA, every grant names a
slot as well as a channel.

41
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:43,124
Full write-ups linked below.
